# Line endings (B-81)
#
# Every commit on this repo printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time
# Git touches it" for each staged file. Harmless, and it trains you to skim the
# one place git speaks up — which is the same failure mode `claims_lint` exists
# to prevent, one layer down.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide per file: normalise to LF in the repository, check
# out in the platform's native ending. Nothing about the working tree changes on
# Windows; what stops is the warning and the risk of a whole-file diff the day
# someone commits from a different machine.
* text=auto

# Windows batch files are run by cmd.exe, which wants CRLF. The scheduled jobs
# (scanner_daily, opportunity_daily, alert_watcher, sentiment_daily) are
# generated with explicit \r\n by `scheduler_core.write_bat` for exactly this
# reason; pinning it here keeps a checkout from undoing that.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Leave data and binaries alone. A CSV that git "normalises" is a CSV whose
# bytes no longer match the hash anything downstream recorded.
*.csv  -text
*.xlsx binary
*.db   binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.epub binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
